titre $ KEY TO THE TAXA OF ARRHENIA IN THE IBERIAN PENINSULA
com$tirée de http://www.mycologia.org/cgi/content/full/95/4/700, adaptée GF mai 08
1,2 $ Species with clamp connections
1,5 $ Species without clamp connections
2,3 $ Basidiomata pleurotoid, with very short or absent stipe, lamellae well developed. Spores broadly ellipsoidal to elongate, Q = 1.5-2.0 
2,4 $ Basidiomata pleurotoid, stipe present or absent, lamellae forked, reduced to vein-like or rib-like wrinkles. Spores broadly ellipsoidal to ellipsoidal, Q = 1.3-1.7
3,0 $ Basidia tetrasporic. Spores 8-10(-11) x (4-)5-6.5 µm, Q = 1.5-1.7 $ A. acerosa var. acerosa
3,0 $ Basidia bisporic, occasionally mono-, tri- or tetrasporic. Spores 10-13.5 x 5-6 µm, Q = [type: 1.8-]2.0 $ A. acerosa var. tenella
4,0 $ Basidiomata dorsally or laterally attached, stipe absent. Spores broadly ellipsoidal, Q = 1.3. On mosses in peaty sites $ A. lobata
4,0 $ Basidiomata stipitate, hymenophore well delimited from a solid stipe by a sterile lateral margin.. Spores ellipsoidal to slightly elongate, Q = 1.4-1.7. On soil among mosses $ A. auriscalpium
5,0 $ Basidiomata cupulate, dorsally attached, stipe absent, hymenophore delimited by a sterile margin. Spores ellipsoidal to oval, Q = 1.6. Usually on pleurocarpous mosses $ A. retiruga
5,0 $ Basidiomata pleurotoid, with a lateral and well-developed stipe continuous with the hymenophore. Spores elongate or lacrimiform, Q = 1.7. Usually on acrocarpous mosses $ A. spathulata
